            <fr:title text="Rich Forest Rendering in Emacs">Rich Forest Rendering in Emacs</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>TODO</fr:taxon>
            <fr:meta name="status">icebox</fr:meta>
            <fr:meta name="apple-id">x-apple-reminder://8A6A3CD1-568E-44B2-9A99-7C2C63A63F9B</fr:meta>
            <fr:meta name="forester-addr">sss-008X</fr:meta>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>Part of <fr:link href="/sss-008V/" title="Modern Web Browser from Within Emacs" uri="https://stevenschaefer.net/sss-008V/" display-uri="sss-008V" type="local">Modern Web Browser from Within Emacs</fr:link>. Supersedes the text-rendering half of <fr:link href="/sss-003B/" title="Tree Rendering in Emacs" uri="https://stevenschaefer.net/sss-003B/" display-uri="sss-003B" type="local">Tree Rendering in Emacs</fr:link> by aiming instead for full HTML-quality rendering inside an Emacs buffer.</html:p>
            <html:p>Goal: when previewing a tree in Emacs, show a rendering that matches what the Forester-compiled HTML shows in a real browser — rendered math, transclusions expanded inline, working links, Datalog query results, figure images — rather than the plain-text-with-markup view we have today.</html:p>
            <html:p>For a demonstration of what current rendering looks like versus what rich rendering would produce, see <fr:link href="/sss-008Y/" title="Example: Current vs. Rich Rendering" uri="https://stevenschaefer.net/sss-008Y/" display-uri="sss-008Y" type="local">Example: Current vs. Rich Rendering</fr:link>.</html:p>
            <html:p>Implementation routes:</html:p>
            <html:ul><html:li><html:strong>Embed the forester dev server output directly.</html:strong> We already serve the rendered forest at <html:code>http://localhost:1313</html:code>. An embedded browser (EAF / <html:code>xwidget-webkit</html:code>) could visit the URL for the current tree. This is the cheapest path: no new rendering code, and every Forester feature (math, Datalog, transclusions, themes) works out of the box.</html:li>
<html:li><html:strong>Parse tree + render to a rich Emacs buffer.</html:strong> Convert Forester markup to overlays, images (for math), and text properties in a native buffer. Keeps everything Emacs-native but requires reimplementing a nontrivial fraction of the compiler.</html:li>
<html:li><html:strong>Hybrid.</html:strong> Use native buffer for editing, embedded browser in a side window for preview. Auto-refresh on save.</html:li></html:ul>
            <html:p>Open questions:</html:p>
            <html:ul><html:li>Does <html:code>xwidget-webkit</html:code> reliably handle KaTeX / MathJax? Forester uses KaTeX; if the embedded browser can't run it, math renders as raw source.</html:li>
<html:li>How do we round-trip clicks in the embedded rendering back to "open this tree in Emacs"? The existing <html:code>[edit]</html:code> button (see <fr:link href="/sss-003H/" title="Open Editor" uri="https://stevenschaefer.net/sss-003H/" display-uri="sss-003H" type="local">Open Editor</fr:link>) already goes the other way.</html:li>
<html:li>Server dependency: option 1 assumes a live <html:code>./server.sh</html:code>. Fine on my laptop; less fine if I open a tree on a machine without the forester toolchain.</html:li></html:ul>
          </fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
      </fr:mainmatter>
    </fr:tree>
